CFBundle: a .kext's executable is in Contents/MacOS even when the host is Linux

Applies to swift-corelibs-foundation (Sources/CoreFoundation), not to dyld.

_CFBundleGetPlatformExecutablesSubdirectoryName() answers "which
Contents/<X>/ holds this bundle's executable" from the *host* OS, and returns
"Linux" under TARGET_OS_LINUX.  That is right for a Linux bundle and wrong for
everything this tool does: dyld_app_cache_util reads Apple's .kext bundles,
whose executables are always in Contents/MacOS, and it does so through
CFBundleCopyExecutableURL().

Symptom without this patch: the build succeeds, quietly.  Every kext looks
codeless, so nothing is linked in and the collection comes out with a single
LC_FILESET_ENTRY (com.apple.kernel) instead of one per kext -- while
__PRELINK_INFO still lists every bundle, because that half comes from the
Info.plists.
diff --git a/Sources/CoreFoundation/CFBundle_Resources.c b/Sources/CoreFoundation/CFBundle_Resources.c
index f32ce6d..1325300 100644
--- a/Sources/CoreFoundation/CFBundle_Resources.c
+++ b/Sources/CoreFoundation/CFBundle_Resources.c
@@ -515,6 +515,11 @@ CF_EXPORT CFStringRef _CFBundleGetCurrentPlatform(void) {
 }
 
 CF_PRIVATE CFStringRef _CFBundleGetPlatformExecutablesSubdirectoryName(void) {
+    // base/darwin/toolchain/build-dyld-app-cache-util.sh (Darwin-bench): this CoreFoundation reads *macOS*
+    // bundles (.kext) on a Linux host, so the executable always lives in
+    // Contents/MacOS, never Contents/Linux.  Without this every kext looks
+    // codeless and the collection comes out with the kernel and nothing else.
+    return CFSTR("MacOS");
 #if TARGET_OS_MAC
     return CFSTR("MacOS");
 #elif TARGET_OS_WIN32
